WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.869
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar

00:00:01.869 --> 00:00:05.758
Five people are seated randomly around a circular table.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.859
What is the probability two people will be seated next to each other?

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:18.720
We can make the question a little bit more precise by labeling the people as A, B, C, D and E

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.860
and asking about the probability that A and B will be seated next to each other?

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.500
This is part one of the question

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:35.520
The next part is to ask for the same probability if the five people are seated randomly in a row.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.280
The next part is to generalize

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.820
What is the probability if there are n people seated randomly?

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.800
Solve for both cases of seating around a circular table and in a row.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.400
This problem has been asked to programmers

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.340
and during other technical jobs as an interview question.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.360
Can you figure it out?

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.100
Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for the solution.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.180
We'll first consider seating around a circular table with five people

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:24.300
There are a couple of ways we'll solve this the first method is will consider equally likely seats

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.480
We have a circular table with five different seats

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.940
Person A will be in some seat.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.260
Without loss of generality we can make it the seat at the top of the diagram

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:42.360
Person B can be in any of the four remaining seats.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.920
It's equally likely that b will be in any of these seats.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:54.200
All these seats exactly two of them will be adjacent to A.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:00.900
Therefore the probability that A and B will be seated adjacent

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.520
is 2/4 which equals 1/2 and that's 50%

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:12.460
There's another way you could solve the problem by counting pairs of seats

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.680
When there are five seats there are 5 choose 2 which equals 10 pairs of seats.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.780
Out of these, there are 5 pairs that are adjacent

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:31.920
Each seat can be paired adjacently to the one that's right next to it going Clockwise

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.140
Therefore the probability that A and B will be adjacent

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.340
will be 5/10 which also equals 1/2 and that's also 50%

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.600
Let's now solve the problem of seating in a row

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.640
One way to solve this is to use conditional probability

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.220
There are two different ways that a could be seated

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.260
Person A could be in the first or the last seat.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.000
These are two out of five possible seats.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.420
So if person A is in the first seat for example

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.000
Then person B will be adjacent for exactly one of the four remaining seats

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.360
Similarly a person is at the end seat person B has to be right next to it.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.340
That's one out of the four remaining seats.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.100
Now there's another case that person a could be in a middle seat,

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.480
so in this five person case there are three middle seats.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.160
In any of these seats

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.600
person B can be adjacent to be either on the left side or the right side

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.220
there are two places that person B could be and could be adjacent.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.780
So if we put these cases together

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:53.340
the probability is (2/5)*(1/4) Plus (3/5)*(2/4)

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.980
And that simplifies to be 2/5 which is 40%

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:04.500
Another way is that we could count the pairs of seats. There are 5 choose 2 which is 10 pairs of seats.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.580
Now how many of these are adjacent?

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.200
Well there are 4 pairs of adjacent seats.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:15.400
Because the first the second the third and the fourth seats could be paired with the seat after it

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.500
Therefore there are 4 out of 10 possibilities which also equals 40%

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.260
Now let's generalize for n people

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.240
We could solve these in the same ways

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.340
Let's consider the method of equally likely seat

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.440
Person A will be at some seat.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.400
And now person B can be any of the n-1 remaining seats,

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.400
and it's equally likely that B will be at any of these n-1 remaining seats

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:47.740
Out of them

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:52.800
exactly 2 will be adjacent the seat to the left and the seat to the right of person A

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.909
Therefore there will be exactly 2/(n-1)

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:07.240
possible ways that A and B could be next to each other. So the probability is 2/(n-1)

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.760
We can also do the same thing by counting the pairs of seats

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.440
There are n choose 2 which equals n(n-1) /2 pairs of seats

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:25.460
Out of these there are n pairs of adjacent seats we start from the top of this diagram and go clockwise

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:32.920
Each seat can be paired with the one right after it we end up counting n different pairs of adjacent seats

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:40.760
So we take n divided by n choose 2, this also simplifies to be 2/(n-1)

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.980
Let's now solve the case seating in a row where you have n people

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.940
We can again solve it by conditional probability

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.860
One case is that A is either in the first seat or the last seat?

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.840
So these will be 2 out of the n possible seats, so in the first seat

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.400
person B could be adjacent for only 1/(n-1) remaining seats

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:10.840
Would have to be just right after or if a was at the very end B would have to be just before.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:15.880
Now there's also the other case that A could be in a middle seat

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:20.240
there are (n-2)/n middle seats

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.940
Any of these middle seats B could either be on the left side or the right side.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:31.400
So there will be 2/(n-1) possible ways that B could be adjacent

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:37.320
if we combine these cases we end up with the following equation.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:44.460
(2/n)*(1/(n-1)) + ((n-2)/n)*(2/(n-1))

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:50.520
If you work through the algebra this simplifies to be 2/n, and that's the probability

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.800
We also do the same thing by counting pairs of seats

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:01.980
There n choose 2 pairs of seats and now how many adjacent seats are there? Well there are n-1

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:08.040
Each seat can be paired with the seat to its right so starting from the first seat on the left

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:14.220
We can count all the way up to n-1 that very last seat has no seat to its right

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:22.540
Therefore, we take n-1 and divide it by n choose 2 and that also simplifies to be 2/n

